# Patches must survive a checkout byte-exact.
#
# Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites LF to CRLF in
# the working tree. That silently corrupts every patch in this repo: `git apply`
# and `patch` then fail on line endings alone, with a symptom that reads like a
# malformed diff rather than a checkout artefact. `-text` disables the
# conversion in both directions.
#
# This is the same class of problem that stops gcc 2.95 compiling CRLF sources
# on the PS2 -- a backslash followed by CR is not a line continuation -- and it
# cost an afternoon there before it was recognised. Not twice.
*.patch -text
*.diff -text

# Shell scripts are run by bash, in a container or on a PS2. They need LF even
# when the checkout happens on Windows.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Build inputs the toolchain reads. Makefiles in particular: a CRLF line ending
# becomes part of a variable's value, so a path silently gains a stray \r.
Makefile text eol=lf
*.mk text eol=lf
